// The lights a room has, as against the ones a street has.
//
//     import 'indoor-lights' { TableLamp, FloorLamp, Pendant, Sconce }
//
//     object TableLamp(0.55)                       // lit, because a room light usually is
//     object FloorLamp(1.6, 2)  { translate X 2 }
//     object Pendant(0.9)       { translate Y 2.6 }   // hangs from where you put it
//     object Sconce(0.30)       { translate [1, 1.7, 0] }
//
// | `TableLamp` | A base with a shade on it, for a table or a sideboard. |
// | `FloorLamp` | The same light on a stem, standing on the floor. |
// | `Pendant`   | A shade or a globe on a flex, hanging from the ceiling. |
// | `Sconce`    | A half shade against a wall, throwing its light up it. |
//
// **The first number is a size**, as it is everywhere but the vehicles -- a height for the two that
// stand, the drop from the ceiling for a `Pendant`, and how tall the fitting is for a `Sconce`.
// **The second is which one of that kind**, as it always is.
//
// There is no season here.  Nothing indoors knows what month it is, and a parameter that never means
// anything is worse than one that is missing, so these take `variant` where the outdoor fittings take
// `season` and it is the *second* number rather than the third.
//
// -- `lit` is on by default here, and that is the opposite of outdoors --------------------------------
//
// `outdoor-lights` ships its lamps dark, because most scenes with a street lamp in them are daylight
// scenes where the lamp is off and should cost nothing.  Indoors that reasoning runs backwards: a room
// is a room because something in it is lit, and an interior with every fitting dark is not a scene of a
// room at night, it is a scene of nothing at all.  So `lit` is `1` unless you say otherwise.
//
// It is not free.  A fitting here lights the way `fire`'s flames and the street lamps do -- an emissive
// `medium` inside a shell, marked `gives light` -- which is the honest way, because the thing you can
// see glowing and the thing doing the lighting are one object and cannot disagree.  It also costs about
// eleven times a plain point light, and it scales with how many are burning.  Four fittings alight in
// one room is four times that, so light a room with one or two and let the rest be dark glass.
//
// -- The shade is the whole difficulty ----------------------------------------------------------------
//
// A lantern's problem is that it must be mostly *glass*: build one as a solid of revolution and the
// globe sits inside something opaque, lighting nothing.  A lampshade is the same problem asking for the
// opposite answer, because a shade is *supposed* to block the light -- sideways.  It is open at the top
// and the bottom, and what a table lamp actually does is throw two cones, one at the ceiling and one at
// the table, with the shade itself glowing softly between them.
//
// So the shade here is a real open-ended cone with a translucent material on it.  `transparency` is what
// gets through and `interior { filter }` is what colour it comes out, and a shadow ray obeys both, so
// the light that crosses the shade arrives dimmed and warmed while the light out of the ends arrives
// whole.  Measured on a wall beside a lamp, that part is exactly linear in the number: 0.07, 0.18 and
// 0.40 gave 1.95, 4.95 and 10.92, while the wall *above* stayed at 64.2 throughout, the up-cone coming
// out of the open top and not caring what the linen does.
//
// **What the transparency does not do is make the shade glow**, which is worth knowing because it is the
// obvious guess and it is wrong.  Set the linen to 0.001 -- opaque in all but name -- and the shade still
// reads 220 of 255.  An open surface here is lit from *either* side, so the bulb inside lights the shade
// and what you see from outside is that shading, not the bulb through it.  Which is a gift: the glow
// costs nothing and is not a knob, so `transparency` can be chosen entirely for how much light should
// cross to the room, and 0.28 is a linen that lights the wall it stands against.
//
// -- What is here for the library and not for you -----------------------------------------------------
//
// Everything beginning with `Room` is the machinery, carrying the prefix for the reason the other
// libraries give: a material is looked up where it is *used*, so these arrive whether you asked or not.
// The prefix differs from `outdoor-lights`' `Lamp` so that a scene may import both.

// -- What a fitting is made of ------------------------------------------------------------------------

RoomBrass = material {
    pigment [0.60, 0.46, 0.20]
    specular 0.62  shininess 150  reflective 0.16  ambient 0.06
}
RoomBronze = material {
    pigment [0.20, 0.16, 0.12]
    specular 0.44  shininess 90  reflective 0.10  ambient 0.05
}
RoomSteel = material {
    pigment [0.42, 0.44, 0.46]
    specular 0.58  shininess 130  reflective 0.20  ambient 0.06
}
RoomCeramic = material {
    pigment [0.78, 0.74, 0.68]
    specular 0.40  shininess 70  ambient 0.08
}

// The flex, and the rose it comes out of.  A cord reads as a cord because it is nearly black, not
// because it is thick.
RoomFlex = material { pigment [0.09, 0.09, 0.10]  specular 0.20  shininess 30  ambient 0.04 }

// -- The shade ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Linen over a frame: pale, and about half of what falls on it gets through, warmed on the way.  Those
// two numbers are what make a shade a shade rather than a bucket -- see the note at the top.
RoomLinen = material {
    pigment [0.92, 0.86, 0.74]
    specular 0.06  shininess 12  ambient 0.10
    transparency 0.28
    interior { ior 1.0  filter 0.85 }
}

// A darker shade, for a fitting meant to throw its light rather than spread it.
RoomShadeDark = material {
    pigment [0.34, 0.26, 0.19]
    specular 0.10  shininess 20  ambient 0.06
    transparency 0.16
    interior { ior 1.0  filter 0.90 }
}

// Opal glass, which is what a pendant globe is: more of it gets through than linen, and it scatters
// rather than colours.
RoomOpal = material {
    pigment [0.95, 0.94, 0.90]
    specular 0.30  shininess 80  ambient 0.12
    transparency 0.62
    interior { ior 1.05  filter 0.30 }
}

primitive RoomShadeOf(variant) -> material {
    switch (mod(floor(variant), 3)) {
        case 0 { return material RoomLinen }
        case 1 { return material RoomLinen }
        default { return material RoomShadeDark }
    }
}

primitive RoomMetalOf(variant) -> material {
    switch (mod(floor(variant), 3)) {
        case 0 { return material RoomBrass }
        case 1 { return material RoomSteel }
        default { return material RoomBronze }
    }
}

// -- The burning part ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Tungsten, and a colder modern lamp.  A room lit by the first is the one people mean by "warm".
RoomTungsten = pigment [1.00, 0.82, 0.56]
RoomCool = pigment [0.94, 0.96, 1.00]

primitive RoomGlowOf(variant) -> pigment {
    switch (mod(floor(variant), 3)) {
        case 1 { return RoomCool }
        default { return RoomTungsten }
    }
}

// **A bulb is a bulb**, and this is the number that says so.  Every fitting here uses the same one
// rather than a bulb sized as a fraction of itself, and that is not tidiness -- it is the only way the
// fittings can agree about how bright they are.  What a volume light delivers goes as the *volume* of
// the emitting stuff, so a bulb scaled to its fitting makes a floor lamp several times a table lamp and
// a pendant several times again, from the same density.  The first cut of this library did exactly that
// and the pendant and the sconce blew out while the table lamp sat at a third of them.  Three centimetres
// of radius is about a real bulb, and it fits inside the smallest shade here with room to spare.
RoomBulbSize = 0.030

// The shell an emissive medium lives in.
RoomShell = material { pigment White  ambient 0  diffuse 0  specular 0  transparency 1 }

// The bulb: a shell with an emissive medium in it, told to light what is around rather than only itself.
// When `lit` is nought it is nothing at all.
//
// **The density constant is not the street lamps'**, and it cannot be.  What a volume light delivers
// goes as the emission times the *volume* of the stuff, and a bulb is a tenth the radius of a lantern's
// globe -- a thousandth the volume.  A room is also a tenth the size of a street, which the inverse
// square hands back, but not all of it.  The number below was measured on a wall two units off, not
// guessed from the outdoor one.
primitive RoomBulb(size, lit, variant, samples) -> group {
    switch (floor(lit)) {
        case 0 { return group { } }
        default {
            return group {
                sphere {
                    material RoomShell {
                        interior {
                            ior 1.0
                            medium {
                                emission pigment RoomGlowOf(variant)
                                density function {
                                    max(0, 1 - sqrt(x * x + y * y + z * z)) * 52000
                                }
                            }
                        }
                    }
                    no shadow
                    gives light samples samples
                    scale size
                }
            }
        }
    }
}

// The bulb you can see when the fitting is out: pale glass, never dark.  A dark bulb reads as a hole in
// the fitting, which is the commonest way an unlit one goes wrong.
RoomBulbCold = material {
    pigment [0.86, 0.86, 0.83]
    specular 0.40  shininess 110  ambient 0.10
}

primitive RoomColdBulb(size, lit) -> group {
    switch (floor(lit)) {
        case 0 { return group { sphere { material RoomBulbCold  scale size } } }
        default { return group { } }
    }
}

// A shade, as a real open-ended surface of revolution: `low` across at the bottom, `high` at the top,
// `tall` from one to the other, sitting with its bottom rim on Y nought.
//
// A conic's radius *is* its distance from the origin along Y, so the section from -low to -high is
// already the frustum wanted -- wider below, narrower above -- and scaling Y stretches it to the height
// asked for without touching either radius.  `open` is what leaves the ends as holes, and the ends are
// where the light gets out.
primitive RoomShade(low, high, tall, variant) -> group {
    return group {
        conic {
            material RoomShadeOf(variant)
            min Y -low  max Y -high
            open
            scale [1, tall / (low - high), 1]
            translate Y low * tall / (low - high)
        }
    }
}

// -- A table lamp ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// A base with a shade on it, standing on whatever you put it on.  `height` is the whole fitting, foot to
// the top of the shade.
primitive TableLamp(height, variant = 1, lit = 1) -> group {
    shadeTall = height * 0.38
    shadeLow = height * 0.30
    shadeHigh = height * 0.20

    return group {
        // The foot, and the turned body above it.
        cylinder {
            material RoomMetalOf(variant)  min Y 0  max Y 1
            scale [height * 0.15, height * 0.025, height * 0.15]
        }
        lathe {
            path {
                move to 0, 0
                line to 0.90, 0
                quad 1.00, 0.55 to 0.55, 1.10
                line to 0.34, 2.00
                line to 0.30, 2.60
                line to 0, 2.62
            }
            material RoomCeramic
            scale height * 0.14
            translate Y height * 0.025
        }
        // The stem above the body, which is what the shade appears to sit on.
        cylinder {
            material RoomMetalOf(variant)  min Y 0  max Y 1
            scale [height * 0.012, height * 0.24, height * 0.012]
            translate Y height * 0.38
        }

        object RoomColdBulb(RoomBulbSize, lit) { translate Y height * 0.76 }
        object RoomBulb(RoomBulbSize, lit, variant, 20) { translate Y height * 0.76 }

        object RoomShade(shadeLow, shadeHigh, shadeTall, variant)
            { translate Y height - shadeTall }
    }
}

// A bowl: the shade's trick the other way up, `low` across at the bottom and `high` at the top, so it
// throws its light at the ceiling instead of at the floor.  A conic's radius is its distance from the
// origin along Y, so this is the section from `low` to `high` on the *positive* side where the shade
// takes the negative one.
primitive RoomBowl(low, high, tall, variant) -> group {
    return group {
        conic {
            material RoomMetalOf(variant)
            min Y low  max Y high
            open
            scale [1, tall / (high - low), 1]
            translate Y -low * tall / (high - low)
        }
    }
}

// -- A floor lamp -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// The same light on a stem.  `height` is the whole fitting, and a standard lamp is mostly stem: the
// shade is about a fifth of it, where a table lamp's is nearer two fifths, which is the difference
// between the two silhouettes more than any detail is.
primitive FloorLamp(height, variant = 1, lit = 1) -> group {
    shadeTall = height * 0.20
    shadeLow = height * 0.16
    shadeHigh = height * 0.11

    return group {
        // A foot wide enough to look like it would not go over.
        lathe {
            path {
                move to 0, 0
                line to 1.00, 0
                line to 0.96, 0.16
                quad 0.40, 0.22 to 0.16, 0.42
                line to 0, 0.44
            }
            material RoomMetalOf(variant)
            scale height * 0.11
        }
        cylinder {
            material RoomMetalOf(variant)  min Y 0  max Y 1
            scale [height * 0.011, height * 0.82, height * 0.011]
            translate Y height * 0.04
        }
        // A finial on top, which is what stops it looking like a stick with a hat.  It goes *above* the
        // shade rather than around it: a ring at the shade's waist has to be narrower than the shade is
        // there, and the first cut of this was `height * 0.105` against a shade measuring about 0.135 --
        // so it hooped straight out through the linen and read as a bright wire across the middle.
        sphere {
            material RoomMetalOf(variant)
            scale height * 0.014
            translate Y height + height * 0.010
        }

        object RoomColdBulb(RoomBulbSize, lit) { translate Y height * 0.895 }
        object RoomBulb(RoomBulbSize, lit, variant, 20) { translate Y height * 0.895 }

        object RoomShade(shadeLow, shadeHigh, shadeTall, variant)
            { translate Y height - shadeTall }
    }
}

// -- A pendant --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Hangs from wherever you put it, which is the ceiling, and reaches `drop` below it.  Unlike everything
// else here its origin is at the *top*: a ceiling is a plane you already know the height of, and having
// to work out where a fitting's foot would be in order to hang it is exactly the sum a library should
// be doing for you.
//
// Variant 1 is an opal globe rather than a shade, and it is worth knowing why that works at all: a globe
// encloses its bulb completely, so every ray out of it crosses the glass.  It lights the room because
// opal is *translucent* -- `transparency 0.62` -- and a shadow ray is dimmed by that rather than stopped.
// Build the same globe out of anything opaque and the fitting goes dark, which is the trap the street
// lanterns fell into from the other side.
// The part that differs between a pendant with a shade and one with a globe.  It is a primitive of its
// own because a `switch` chooses what a primitive *gives back*, so it cannot sit inside a `group` that is
// already being returned -- the choice has to be made one level up and the result dropped in.
primitive RoomPendantLight(drop, variant, lit) -> group {
    switch (mod(floor(variant), 2)) {
        case 1 {
            return group {
                sphere { material RoomOpal  scale drop * 0.22 }
                object RoomColdBulb(RoomBulbSize, lit)
                object RoomBulb(RoomBulbSize, lit, variant, 22)
            }
        }
        default {
            return group {
                object RoomColdBulb(RoomBulbSize, lit) { translate Y drop * 0.02 }
                object RoomBulb(RoomBulbSize, lit, variant, 22) { translate Y drop * 0.02 }
                object RoomShade(drop * 0.30, drop * 0.19, drop * 0.26, variant)
                    { translate Y -drop * 0.22 }
            }
        }
    }
}

primitive Pendant(drop, variant = 1, lit = 1) -> group {
    return group {
        // The rose against the ceiling, and the flex down from it.
        cylinder {
            material RoomMetalOf(variant)  min Y -1  max Y 0
            scale [drop * 0.10, drop * 0.035, drop * 0.10]
        }
        cylinder {
            material RoomFlex  min Y -1  max Y 0
            scale [drop * 0.010, drop * 0.72, drop * 0.010]
            translate Y -drop * 0.03
        }

        object RoomPendantLight(drop, variant, lit) { translate Y -drop * 0.78 }
    }
}

// -- A sconce ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// A bowl on a wall, throwing its light up it -- an uplighter, which is the wall fitting that actually
// lights a room rather than merely marking a place on the wall.  It faces `-Z` like everything that goes
// on a wall, so it is placed the way a window or a `WallLantern` is, and `size` is how tall it stands.
primitive Sconce(size, variant = 1, lit = 1) -> group {
    return group {
        // The back plate against the wall, and the arm out to the bowl.
        lathe {
            path {
                move to 0, 0
                line to 0.44, 0
                quad 0.50, 0.30 to 0.40, 0.62
                line to 0, 0.66
            }
            material RoomMetalOf(variant)
            scale [size * 0.42, size * 0.62, size * 0.10]
            rotate X -90
        }
        cube {
            material RoomMetalOf(variant)
            scale [size * 0.05, size * 0.05, size * 0.24]
            translate [0, 0, -size * 0.22]
        }

        object RoomColdBulb(RoomBulbSize, lit) { translate [0, size * 0.20, -size * 0.42] }
        object RoomBulb(RoomBulbSize, lit, variant, 18) { translate [0, size * 0.20, -size * 0.42] }

        // The bottom hole is narrower than the bulb on purpose: an uplighter that leaks downwards
        // is a downlighter with extra steps.
        object RoomBowl(size * 0.06, size * 0.36, size * 0.34, variant)
            { translate [0, size * 0.01, -size * 0.42] }
    }
}
